      "id": 1,
      "text": "Grand Lake Theatre opened March 6 1926 and originally showed vaudeville and silent films before switching to talkies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Lake_Theatre",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The theater's interior is Art Deco and features neoclassical faux columns, urns, a crystal chandelier, and classical frescos.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Lake_Theatre",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A Mighty Wurlitzer organ hidden beneath the floor rises for a brief concert before each movie on Friday and Saturday evenings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Lake_Theatre",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1980 Renaissance Rialto, Inc. bought the lease and spent millions renovating the theater, adding a second auditorium and later a third and fourth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25,
        26,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Lake_Theatre",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The theater’s rooftop sign, originally made by Brumfield Electric Sign Co., Inc., still illuminates Grand Lake’s skyline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
